In view of \eqref{eKKnhf74g15t}, there exists
$\delta\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$ such that
\begin{equation}
\label{4:e31}
(\forall x\in C)(\forall y\in C)\quad 
\|x-y\|\ensuremath{\leqslant}\delta\quad\Rightarrow\quad\|Tx-Ty\|\ensuremath{\leqslant} 1.
\end{equation}
Now fix $z\in C$ and $\rho\in\ensuremath{\left]0,+\infty\right[}$ such that 
$C\subset\menge{x\in\ensuremath{{\mathcal X}}}{\|x-z\|\ensuremath{\leqslant}\rho}$, and take
an integer $m\ensuremath{\geqslant} 1+\rho/\delta$. 
Let $x\in C$ and set 
\begin{equation}
(\forall n\in\{0,\ldots,m\})\quad  
x_n=x+\dfrac{n}{m}(z-x)\in C.
\end{equation}
Then, for every $n\in\{0,\ldots,m-1\}$,
$\|x_{n+1}-x_n\|=\|z-x\|/m\ensuremath{\leqslant}\rho/m\ensuremath{\leqslant}\delta$ and
\eqref{4:e31} yields $\|Tx_{n+1}-Tx_n\|\ensuremath{\leqslant} 1$. Hence,
$\|Tz-Tx\|\ensuremath{\leqslant}\sum_{n=0}^{m-1}\|Tx_{n+1}-Tx_n\|\ensuremath{\leqslant} m$ and
