\begin{proof}
	If $c \leq 0$, then it is easy to see that $\varphi$ is bounded, since elements of bounded height in a Siegel domain form a compact subset and $\varphi$ is continuous. The same argument shows that if $c<0$ we can assume $\norm[\varphi(g)] \ll {\rm Ht}(g)^c$ to hold in a whole Siegel domain $S$ containing a fundamental domain. If ${\rm Ht}(g)$ is small, we take $\gamma \in {\rm GL}_2(\mathbf{F})$ such that $\gamma g \in S$, thus by the lemma we get
	$$ \norm[\varphi(g)] = \norm[\varphi(\gamma g)] \ll {\rm Ht}(\gamma g)^c \leq {\rm Ht}(g)^{-c}. $$
