where $K_\ell^-$ is the graph obtained from $K_\ell$ by removing an edge.\footnote{Here $o(1)$ denotes a function which tends to $0$ as $t \to \infty$.}
\end{itemize}
\label{general}
Let $\ell,k \in \mathbb{N}$ and $3 \leq k \leq \ell$. Then, whenever $t$ is sufficiently large, the following hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] For all graphs $F$ containing a copy of $K_k$, we have that $s_t(F,k) \geq 2^{t^{c'(k)}}$, where $c'(k) \geq \frac{1}{2(k-2)}$.
\item[(ii)] For all $K_k$-free graphs $F$, we have that $s_t(F,k) \leq 2^\ell(t+2)^{e(F)+2\ell}$.
\end{itemize}
A weaker conjecture, asking whether minimum degree $d$ forces exponential in $d$ many distinguishable cycles, was verified by Tuza~\cite{tuza2} who obtained a lower bound $2^{\lfloor(d+4)/2\rfloor}-O(d^2)$ for graphs with average degree $d$.

