by integration by parts. Our goal is to prove that $G(0)\ge (1+\gamma)^{-1/\gamma}$.

The function $G^\gamma$ is concave for $\gamma<0$. Thus it lies above its tangent at $0$. This means that $G(x)\le G(0)(1+\gamma cx)^\frac{1}{\gamma}$, where $c=G'(0)/G(0)>0$. We now set $K$ large enough so that $1/c<K$. Then
\begin{equation*}
G(x)\le
\begin{cases}
G(0)(1+\gamma cx)^\frac{1}{\gamma}, & \mbox{if}\ x\le 1/c,\\
1, & \mbox{if}\ x> 1/c.
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
So
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
K&=\int_{-K}^KG(x)dx\\
&\le \int_{-K}^{1/c}G(0)(1+\gamma cx)^\frac{1}{\gamma}dx+\int_{1/c}^K1dx\\
&=\frac{G(0)}{c(\gamma+1)}[(1+\gamma)^{\frac{1}{\gamma}+1}-(1-\gamma cK)^{\frac{1}{\gamma}+1}]+K-\frac{1}{c}\\
&\le\frac{G(0)(1+\gamma)^{\frac{1}{\gamma}}}{c}+K-\frac{1}{c},
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
which implies that $G(0)\ge (1+\gamma)^{-1/\gamma}$ as claimed. Replacing $X$ with $Y=-X$, we obtain the result.

