The maps are isomorphisms for the following reasons.  Map one: $[\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\cdots\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n]$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf (Lemma \ref{associatedgradedhomotopysheaf}), and a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf calculates its own $\check H^\bullet$ (Proposition \ref{homotopysheafcechquasiisomorphism}).  Map two: the map of coefficient $\mathcal K$-presheaves is a quasi-isomorphism (Lemma \ref{cechsimplebasic}) and thus induces an isomorphism on $\check H^\bullet$ (Lemma \ref{qicech}).  Map three: the map of coefficient $\mathcal K$-presheaves is a quasi-isomorphism on stalks (by purity), and thus induces an isomorphism on $\check H^\bullet$ (Proposition \ref{cechdeterminedbystalks}).

It suffices to show the weak vanishing property for $\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Let $\alpha\in H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K)$ be arbitrary with $i<0$.  By Remark \ref{nbhdintersectiongood} and Lemma \ref{anycompactintersectionworks}, the following is a quasi-isomorphism:
