The collection of standard simplices $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}$ equipped with their standard simplicial stratifications and the standard identifications of the faces of $\Delta^n$ with the various $\Delta^i$, forms an MF-set.
\label{flasquehomotopysheafresolution}
A sheaf $\mathcal F$ is \emph{flasque} iff all restriction maps $\mathcal F(U)\to\mathcal F(U')$ are surjective.  It is easy to see that any bounded below complex of flasque sheaves $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy sheaf.  For example, let $\mathcal F^\bullet(U):=C^\bullet(U)$ be the presheaf of singular cochain complexes.  Then the sheafification of $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a complex of flasque sheaves, and thus is a homotopy sheaf.  One can show (using barycentric subdivision) that the map from $\mathcal F^\bullet$ to its sheafification is a quasi-isomorphism, and hence $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy sheaf as well.
\label{softhomotopysheafresolution}
A $\mathcal K$-sheaf $\mathcal F$ is \emph{soft} iff all restriction maps $\mathcal F(K)\to\mathcal F(K')$ are surjective.  It is easy to see that any complex of soft $\mathcal K$-sheaves $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.
The existence of an implicit atlas on $X$ does impose some additional restriction on the topology on $X$.  For example, if $X$ admits an implicit atlas then it is locally metrizable and hence metrizable by Smirnov's theorem (a paracompact Hausdorff locally metrizable space is metrizable).
It is easy to see that if $A$ is locally $S^1$-orientable, then $\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_X$ is locally isomorphic to the constant sheaf $\underline R$ (where $\pi:X\to X/S^1$).
It is easy to see that $H_\bullet(M)$ and $J_\bullet(M)$ are both \emph{contractible Kan complexes}.  A semisimplicial set $Z_\bullet$ is a contractible Kan complex iff every map $\partial\Delta^n\to Z_\bullet$ can be extended to a map $\Delta^n\to Z_\bullet$ for all $n\geq 0$ (where $\Delta^n$ is the semisimplicial $n$-simplex).
This is the best way to prove the desired result, but we can actually get away with a less technical argument.  Namely, it is easy to see that the identifications induced by points with smooth domain curve agree on overlaps (since then there is no gluing and we have a nice smooth Banach bundle picture).  Since the nodal locus is codimension two inside $\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}$ (this follows from the gluing map constructed previously), the identification $\mathfrak o_{\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}}=\mathfrak o_E\otimes\bigotimes_{i=1}^\ell\mathfrak o_{u(p_{n+i})^\ast N_{D/X}}^\vee$ away from the nodal locus extends uniquely to all of $\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}$.

