theorem:
Let $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ be a finite structure whose universe $V(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ has size strictly greater than~$1$. If each
polymorphism of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ is essentially unary, then {\sc Surjective $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$-Colouring}
is \NP-complete.
\label{thm:Chen-hauptsatz}
 Let $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ be  
an endo-trivial 
reflexive digraph with at least three vertices. Then every polymorphism of  $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ is essentially unary.  
\label{cor:Benoit}

Let $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ be a finite structure so that the idempotent polymorphisms of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ omit all Taylor operations. Then 
{\sc $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$-Retraction} is \NP-complete.
\label{thm:ret-hardness}

Let $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ be a non-transitive reflexive tournament. Then {\sc Surjective $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$-Colouring}
is \NP-complete.
\label{thm:hauptsatz}

Let $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ be a partially reflexive digraph of size at most $3$. Then {\sc Surjective $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$-Colouring} is polynomially equivalent to {\sc $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$-Retraction}. In particular, it is always in \Ptime\ or is \NP-complete.
\label{thm:3digraph}
So far it only exists in the work of Chen~\cite{ChenSurHom}, who proved that {\sc Surjective $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$-Colouring} is \NP-complete if $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ 
has the property that all of its polymorphisms depend only on one variable, that is, are essentially unary.

lemma:
\label{l-gg2}
If $(f_1,g_1) \in E(\widehat{\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}^\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}})$ and $(f_2,g_2) \in E(\widehat{\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}^\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}})$, then $((f_1 \circ f_2),(g_1 \circ g_2)) \in E(\widehat{\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}^\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}})$. 
